From 2776b6a8776344f4b217af1dcff8ef6a40d7198f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:16:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AUTO_MEMORIES_REGFILES: register files of placed standard
 cells
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A register file, many ports over few words, is not an SRAM: FakeRAM's
single-port model does not describe it, and synthesised to flops its
mux trees are placed by a placer that does not know they are an array.

AUTO_MEMORIES_REGFILES lists spec files, one per RTL module.
gen_memories.py runs OpenROAD's generate_regfile on each: it checks the
spec against the module's ports and writes the cells (<m>.v), an
abstract whose pins sit where their connections land in the array
(<m>.lef) and a timing model (<m>.lib, <m>_pre_layout.lib) beside the
FakeRAM views. The module joins blackboxes.txt with the area its .lib
states, so synthesis and macro placement see a macro that
SYNTH_MINIMUM_KEEP_SIZE can cost, and memories.json records it with kind
`regfile` (the others get `fakeram` or `flops`).

In `mode netlist` the macro dissolves into its cells at the end of
macro placement (regfile_dissolve.tcl): the core FIRM where the placer
put the macro, flipped as it was, row parity kept; the address decode
left to global placement and the resizer; the core's own cells and
nets dont_touch except the flops (CTS rewires them) and the cells on
the core's boundary (repair_design buffers their nets); then
eliminate_dead_logic, as at synthesis. `mode macro` keeps the macro.

designs/asap7/regfile is the smallest example: one 16x8 2R2W file in a
parent of flops, seconds to global route.

Moving parts into OpenROAD, the dissolve in particular, is left for
later: faster, unit-testable, maintained with the database it edits.

Needs OpenROAD's generate_regfile:
https://github.com/The-OpenROAD-Project/OpenROAD/pull/11669

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Øyvind Harboe <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
